---
title: Beef stew
description: Lesson 3.3. Braising, and what long, moist heat does to a tough cut.
servings: 4
time: 3 hours 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, wet-heat]
lesson: "3.3"
technique: braising
status: draft
---

> Technique: braising. Brown the meat, then cook it slowly, covered,
> in a little liquid. It is for cuts that are tough because they are
> full of connective tissue, and it turns that toughness into
> tenderness.

Heat the oven to 150 °C (130 °C fan).

Pat @beef chuck{800%g}(in 4 cm pieces) dry with kitchen paper and season with @salt{1%tsp}.

> Why this cut: chuck has fat, which gives flavour, and a lot of
> collagen, which makes it tough. Lean, tender cuts have neither and
> dry out in a braise.
-- source: Emily Rahravan, "What Is Beef Chuck?", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-06-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/7285-what-is-beef-chuck

Heat @oil{2%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) in a #casserole{}(heavy, with a lid, safe for hob and oven) over medium-high heat. Brown the beef in two batches, about ~{4%minutes} a batch, turning the pieces until they are dark brown on at least two sides. Lift each batch out onto a plate.

> Why brown, and why in batches: browning is the Maillard reaction,
> sugars reacting with amino acids, and it makes the flavour of the
> stew. It does not seal in juices: in a test, seared and unseared
> steaks lost the same share of their weight. A crowded pot steams the
> meat and nothing browns.
-- source: Molly Birnbaum, "12 Common Cooking Myths, Debunked", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1706-12-common-cooking-myths-debunked (both sets "lost around 22 percent of their weight")

Turn the heat down to medium. Add @onions{2}(chopped) and cook for ~{5%minutes}, until soft. Stir in @tomato paste{1%tbsp} for ~{1%minute}.

Pour in @red wine{250%ml} and scrape the brown layer off the bottom of the pot with a #wooden spoon{}. Add @beef stock{500%ml}, @bay leaf{1} and @thyme{3%sprigs}, return the beef and its juices, and bring to a simmer.

Cover the pot and put it in the oven for ~{2%hours}. Add @carrots{3}(in thick slices), cover again, and cook for ~{45%minutes} more, until a fork slides into the beef with no resistance. Stop there.

> Science: connective tissue is mostly collagen. It begins to break
> down above about 60 °C and does so fastest between about 82 and
> 93 °C, in a moist pot, over hours. Not all of it becomes gelatin: in
> one test kitchen's work on brisket most of the collagen only softened
> enough to make the meat tender. Look up: collagen, gelatin,
> connective tissue, braising.
-- source: "Braising 101", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/6353-braising-101 ; "Getting to Know: Braising", https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/10933-getting-to-know-braising (sweet spot "between 140 and 200 degrees") ; Andrew Janjigian, "The Science Behind Perfect Braised Brisket", America's Test Kitchen, 2017-11-01, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/552-how-to-braise-brisket (fastest "above 180 degrees"; "most of the collagen in brisket doesn't actually break down and convert to gelatin")

> Science: the liquid does not make the meat moist. Muscle proteins set
> and squeeze water out long before the collagen gives way, myosin at
> about 40 to 60 °C and actin at about 70 to 80 °C, and braised meat
> loses weight like any other. The tenderness of a good stew comes
> from collagen softening, not from liquid soaking in. The test
> kitchen's rule follows: cook until a fork slides in, then stop. Look
> up: protein denaturation, myosin, actin.
-- source: Jiang et al., "Molecular mechanisms underlying sensory and chemical changes in muscle foods induced by sous-vide cooking: a review", Foods 14(17):2967, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods14172967 ; Molly Birnbaum, "12 Common Cooking Myths, Debunked", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1706-12-common-cooking-myths-debunked ("Despite the wet conditions, braising adds no moisture") ; "Braising 101", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/6353-braising-101 ; "Getting to Know: Braising", https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/10933-getting-to-know-braising ("Cook braises just until fork-tender, then stop")

Taste the sauce and add salt if it needs it.

> Safety: the cooking is not the risk here: the USDA minimum for whole
> cuts of beef is 63 °C and a braise spends hours above it. The risk
> is afterwards. Cool leftovers as quickly as possible and put them in
> the fridge or freezer.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14 (read from an archive copy), https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart : beef steaks, chops and roasts 145 °F (62.8 °C) ; Food Standards Scotland, "Food storage", https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/consumers/food-safety/at-home/rice

> Next time, without the recipe: a tough, fatty cut, browned in
> batches, then covered with a little liquid and kept just below a
> simmer for hours. Start checking with a fork after two hours.
